{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"exorable-gemstone-bluefish","creation_channel":"mcp","description":"# The defect\n\n`infra/scripts/observability.sh` resolves the two newest cap scripts as siblings of the\nRUNNING probe, using their REPOSITORY basename:\n\n```\n 999: VARTMP_CAP_SH=\"${VARTMP_CAP_SH:-$(cd \"$(dirname \"${BASH_SOURCE[0]}\")\" && pwd)/vartmp-cap.sh}\"\n1092: CONTAINER_CAP_SH=\"${CONTAINER_CAP_SH:-$(cd \"$(dirname \"${BASH_SOURCE[0]}\")\" && pwd)/container-cap.sh}\"\n```\n\n`install-observability.sh:12` installs the probe as `/usr/local/bin/rebar-observability.sh`,\nso `dirname \"${BASH_SOURCE[0]}\"` is `/usr/local/bin` and those expand to\n`/usr/local/bin/vartmp-cap.sh` and `/usr/local/bin/container-cap.sh`.\n\n**Nothing ever creates those two paths.** `install-observability.sh` installs ONLY\n`observability.sh`. The scripts' own `--install` self-installs under a DIFFERENT,\n`rebar-`-prefixed name:\n\n```\ninfra/scripts/vartmp-cap.sh:121    VAR_TMP_INSTALLED_PATH=\"${...:-/usr/local/bin/rebar-vartmp-cap.sh}\"\ninfra/scripts/container-cap.sh:124 CONTAINER_INSTALLED_PATH=\"${...:-/usr/local/bin/rebar-container-cap.sh}\"\n```\n\nSo the name the probe looks for and the name the installer writes can never be the same file.\nThis is a naming mismatch, not a missing deploy step: even a host where\n`vartmp-cap.sh --install` and `container-cap.sh --install` have BOTH run successfully leaves\nthe probe unable to find either script.\n\n## Proven consequences (measured on i-00880b2c7f13527c5, 2026-09-06 01:0xZ)\n\nBoth invocations the probe makes fail with **rc 127**:\n\n```\nbash: /usr/local/bin/vartmp-cap.sh: No such file or directory     vartmp_printenv_rc=127\nbash: /usr/local/bin/container-cap.sh: No such file or directory  container_printenv_rc=127\nVARTMP_CAP_MISSING\nCONTAINER_CAP_MISSING\n```\n\n1. **Two cap-compliance percentages are never published.** `--print-env` produces no output, so\n   `VAR_TMP_MAX_BYTES` / `CONTAINER_WRITABLE_BYTES` stay unset and the budget guards at\n   `observability.sh:1037` and `:1118` are false. `var_tmp_used_percent` and\n   `container_writable_used_percent` are absent from the `rebar/host` namespace entirely —\n   `aws cloudwatch list-metrics --namespace rebar/host` returns `var_tmp_bytes` and\n   `container_writable_bytes` but neither `*_used_percent`, and\n   `rebar-var-tmp-usage-high` / `rebar-container-writable-usage-high` have **0 datapoints in 3h**.\n\n2. **Both heartbeats are pinned to 0 regardless of the true state.** `--check-active` returns an\n   empty string, which `case` coerces to `0`, so `var_tmp_cleanup_active` and\n   `container_reaper_active` publish 0 whether or not a reaper is actually running. The two\n   dead-man alarms read 12/12 datapoints at 0.00.\n\nThe same scripts run from a path where they DO exist answer correctly, which isolates the cause\nto path resolution rather than the scripts:\n\n```\n$ bash /opt/rebar/infra/scripts/vartmp-cap.sh --print-env      # rc=0\nVAR_TMP_MAX_BYTES=4294967296\n$ bash /opt/rebar/infra/scripts/container-cap.sh --print-env   # rc=0\nCONTAINER_WRITABLE_BYTES='2147483648'\n```\n\nThe other two cap scripts are a natural control. `docker-storage-cap.sh` and `journald-cap.sh`\nhappen to be present at `/usr/local/bin` (neither defines an `INSTALLED_PATH`, so their repo\nbasename is the only name they have), and their percentages —\n`docker_storage_used_percent`, `journal_used_percent` — publish normally. Presence at the\nprobe-sibling path is exactly what separates the working pair from the broken pair, 4 for 4.\n\n## What this is hiding\n\n`/var/tmp` measured 3664 MiB against the 4 GiB budget `--print-env` reports — about **89%**,\nabove the 85% threshold of `rebar-var-tmp-usage-high`. The alarm that cannot see its metric\nwould be firing on a true condition if it could.\n\n## Not in scope\n\nThe probe is CURRENT: `sha256(/usr/local/bin/rebar-observability.sh)` equals\n`sha256(origin/main infra/scripts/observability.sh)` = `6d900dbde913...`, so the truncation fix\n`aaa31f11f3bc` is deployed and this is not probe staleness. `probe_truncated` and `probe_ok`\npublish normally.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n1. `observability.sh` locates each cap script at the name its own `--install` writes\n   (`/usr/local/bin/rebar-vartmp-cap.sh`, `/usr/local/bin/rebar-container-cap.sh`) when that\n   file exists, and still finds the repo-sibling `vartmp-cap.sh` / `container-cap.sh` when run\n   from a checkout, so both the installed host and the test/checkout layout resolve.\n2. The `VARTMP_CAP_SH` / `CONTAINER_CAP_SH` environment overrides keep taking precedence over\n   both candidates, so existing tests that inject a stub path are unaffected.\n3. A regression test proves the installed-name layout resolves: with only\n   `rebar-vartmp-cap.sh` present beside the probe (no `vartmp-cap.sh`), a probe run publishes\n   `var_tmp_used_percent`, and the equivalent holds for `container_writable_used_percent`.\n   Reverting the resolution change fails that test.\n4. A test proves the coupling cannot silently break again: the default candidate list in\n   `observability.sh` contains the `*_INSTALLED_PATH` default declared in `vartmp-cap.sh` and\n   `container-cap.sh`, so renaming an installed path without updating the probe fails the build.\n5. `make lint`, `make typecheck` and the affected `tests/scripts/` suites pass.\n\n## Out of scope (file separately)\n\nThat `docker-storage-cap.sh` / `journald-cap.sh` are present at `/usr/local/bin` only by manual\noperator copy — no repo path installs them there — is the same fragility one step removed and\nis tracked on its own ticket.","id":"1e63-bee1-5ce6-4d8f","parent_id":"6202-e1c7-c57f-4897","priority":2,"repos":["rebar"],"tags":[],"ticket_type":"bug","title":"The probe resolves the /var/tmp and container cap scripts by a name nothing ever installs, so two cap-compliance percentages are permanently off the air and both heartbeats read 0 on any host"},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1788657540726195675,"uuid":"29fb21f3-634c-47b9-9645-c825e66213ff"}